@h1 Tables h1@

A Wit table is one node. Hand it rows and it draws a full
@@@code <table> code@@@ — caption, header, body — with no grid of pipes to align.
Rows can come as inline text, as a collection of records, or as @code @row code@ /
@code @col code@ child nodes when cells need links and emphasis. @code table code@
is dispatched to a dedicated renderer, so it does not follow the generic id/class
allowlist — the parameters below are the whole surface.

@h2 Which form? h2@

@table |rows [[You have, Use, Header comes from], [rows typed out as text, inline CSV, row 0 by default], [a collection + column order, schema-as-array, the schema keys], [a collection + custom labels, schema-as-record, the schema labels], [a collection and want auto columns, rows-by-ref, the first record's keys], [cells with links or emphasis, body form (row/col nodes), first row unless header false]]| |caption Pick a form| table@

@h2 Inline rows h2@

Pass a list of rows in the @code rows code@ parameter. Row 0 is the header; every
later row is a body row. A @code caption code@ rides above the table.

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Format, Extension], [Web page, .html], [Markdown, .md], [Print, .pdf]]| |caption Output formats|
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Output formats</caption><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Extension</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Web page</td><td>.html</td></tr><tr><td>Markdown</td><td>.md</td></tr><tr><td>Print</td><td>.pdf</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

Another, with the caption pipe closed before @@@code table@ code@@@:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Client, Hours], [One to One, 62.75], [Couple, 0.75]]| |caption Practicum| table@
→ <table><caption>Practicum</caption><thead><tr><th>Client</th><th>Hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One to One</td><td>62.75</td></tr><tr><td>Couple</td><td>0.75</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/inline-csv.wit code@.

Drop the header with @@@code |header false| code@@@ — the first row becomes a
plain body row:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Web page, .html], [Markdown, .md]]| |header false|
→ <table><tbody><tr><td>Web page</td><td>.html</td></tr><tr><td>Markdown</td><td>.md</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/no-header.wit code@.

@aside(class note) 💡 Inline cells split on commas, and a comma inside a cell
can't be escaped — a stray comma makes an extra cell. Surrounding quotes render
literally. Keep inline cell text comma-free, or use the body form below where each
cell is its own node. aside@

@h2 From a data collection h2@

Point @code rows code@ at a collection of records and each record becomes a row.
With no schema, columns are auto-derived from the @strong first record's keys strong@.
The renderer prefers the expander's typed collection over literal text.

@@@pre
#packages: [ { name - parser, role - reads source }, { name - runtime, role - resolves data } ]

@table |rows @packages|
pre@@@

Emits headings @code name code@ / @code role code@ over the two records. A second
example, with the full emit:

@@@pre
#sales: [ { region - West, total - 100 }, { region - East, total - 90 } ]

@table |rows @sales| table@
→ <table><thead><tr><th>region</th><th>total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>West</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>East</td><td>90</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@aside(class note)
A @strong raw strong@ @@@code @@table code@@@ cannot resolve
@@@code |rows @sales| code@@@ — a raw body is a verbatim Text node, so
resolve and expand never run on it and there is no typed collection to derive
columns from. Use the ordinary @@@code @table code@@@. See
@a |href /docs/reference/limitations/| limitations a@.
aside@

To choose and label the columns yourself, add a schema.

@h3 Schema-as-array (positional) h3@

Schema keys pick record fields @strong in order strong@ and become the headings;
every row is data (no header consumed).

@@@pre
#sites: [{ name - Dunmore Head, status - operational }, { name - Carrick Point, status - maintenance }] !!

@table |schema [name, status]| |rows @sites|
pre@@@

Emits headings @code name code@ / @code status code@ over the two records.
@strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/schema-array.wit code@.

@h3 Schema-as-record (labelled) h3@

The record @strong key strong@ selects the field; the record @strong value strong@
is the column heading.

@@@pre
@table |schema { name - Site, status - Status }| |rows [[Dunmore Head, operational], [Carrick Point, maintenance]]|
pre@@@

Emits headings @code Site code@ / @code Status code@ (not @code name code@ /
@code status code@). The same works pointing at a record collection —
@@@code |schema { name - Package, role - Role }| code@@@ over @@@code @packages code@@@.
@strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/schema-record.wit code@.

Loading the records from a file at build time works the same way; see
@a |href /docs/guides/data/| Data a@.

@h2 Rich cells: the body form h2@

The only form whose cells hold @strong rich content strong@ — links, emphasis,
nested nodes — rather than plain strings. Leave off the @code rows code@ parameter
and the renderer reads the body: a @@@code @row code@@@ per row, a
@@@code @col code@@@ per cell.

@@@pre
@table |caption Modules|
  @row @col Module col@ @col Notes col@ row@
  @row @col Parser col@ @col Reads *source*; see @a |href /docs/reference/syntax/| the syntax a@. col@ row@
table@
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Modules</caption><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parser</td><td>Reads <strong>source</strong>; see <a href="/docs/reference/syntax/">the syntax</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

The first @@@code @row code@@@ is the header — its cells render as
@@@code <th> code@@@ — unless you pass @code header false code@. A
@@@code @th code@@@ cell is always a header cell, even inside a body row. The
HTML-style names @@@code @tr code@@@ / @@@code @td code@@@ / @@@code @th code@@@
work as aliases for @@@code @row code@@@ / @@@code @col code@@@, and the whole row
can sit on one line: @@@code @row @col a col@ @col b col@ row@ code@@@.

@@@code @row code@@@ and @@@code @col code@@@ are @strong interchangeable strong@:
the outer node is the row, the inner one the cell, whichever keyword you use. So
@@@code @col @row a row@ @row b row@ col@ code@@@ builds the same row as
@@@code @row @col a col@ @col b col@ row@ code@@@.

@h3 Data-driven rows with rich cells h3@

This is the form the others can't reach: rows generated from data, with cells that
still hold nodes. Wrap the @@@code @row code@@@ line in
@@@code (each … as …) code@@@ and pull fields into each cell body.

@@@pre
#modules: [ { name - Parser, status - stable }, { name - Runtime, status - stable } ]

@table |caption Modules|
  @row @col Module col@ @col Status col@ row@
  (each @modules as m)
  @row @col @m.name col@ @col @em @m.status em@ col@ row@
  (end)
table@
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Modules</caption><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parser</td><td><em>stable</em></td></tr><tr><td>Runtime</td><td><em>stable</em></td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

The header row stays fixed; the loop appends one body row per record. Put an
@@@code (if …) code@@@ inside the loop to filter which rows appear — here only the
stable modules:

@@@pre
#modules: [ { name - Parser, status - stable }, { name - Sandbox, status - draft } ]

@table |caption Stable modules|
  @row @col Module col@ @col Status col@ row@
  (each @modules as m)
  (if @m.status is stable)
  @row @col @m.name col@ @col @em @m.status em@ col@ row@
  (end)
  (end)
table@
pre@@@

The draft row drops out; only Parser survives:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Stable modules</caption><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parser</td><td><em>stable</em></td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@h2 One limitation: data resolves in the body, not in a parameter h2@

A field reference resolves inside a cell @strong body strong@ — @@@code @col @m.name col@ code@@@
works — but @strong not inside a parameter strong@. A link's @code href code@
cannot yet be @@@code @m.url code@@@:

@@@pre
@col @a |href @m.url| @m.name a@ col@   ✗ the href is not resolved
@col @a |href /modules/parser| @m.name a@ col@   ✓ literal href, data in the text
pre@@@

Keep every data-driven value in the cell body and write parameters as literals.

@h2 Header and caption controls h2@

@table |rows [[Param, Effect], [caption text, prepends a caption element], [header false, no header row], [header N, use row index N as the header], [header [a b c], explicit header cells — all rows become data], [default, row 0 of rows is the header]]| |caption Header controls| table@

@h2 Edge cases and value rules h2@

@table |rows [[Case, Behaviour, Fixture], [empty or missing rows, empty table element, 19 empty], [multi-line cell content, preserved, 19 multiline-cell], [number or boolean cell, String(value), 19 tables], [null cell, empty cell, 19 tables], [comma inside a cell, splits the cell — avoid, verified], [quoting a cell, not supported inline — quotes stay literal, verified]]| |caption Edge cases| table@

@@@pre
@table table@ → <table></table>
pre@@@

@aside(class note)
Two boundaries worth knowing. @strong Inline cells cannot be quoted or
comma-escaped: strong@ a comma inside a cell splits it, and surrounding quotes
render literally. @strong CSV/TSV sources have no quoted-field handling
strong@ (the delimited-source parser), so commas or tabs inside a field break
rows there too; inline @@@code |rows [...]| code@@@ uses the real collection
parser and is unaffected by the source path.
aside@

@h2 Centering is theme, not renderer h2@

The default HTML pathway centres tables via
@@@code .wit-doc table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1em auto } code@@@ in
the theme. @@@code --raw code@@@ ships a reset-only base, so it does not centre —
add your own CSS. @strong Fixture: strong@ @code 19-tables/* code@ and the theme.

